<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adon Olam</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">perhaps 13th or 14th century; in the daily and Shabbat liturgy since the 15th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as אֲדוֹן עוֹלָם</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Adon Olam, &quot;Eternal Lord&quot; or &quot;Sovereign of the Universe&quot;, is a hymn of the Jewish liturgy. Who wrote it, and when, is uncertain. It is sometimes attributed to the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> ibn Gabirol (1021–1058), but there is no solid evidence for this, and its regular metre does not match his other poems; others have named Hai Gaon (939–1038) or even the Talmudic sage Yohanan ben Zakkai.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Adon Olam</b>, &quot;Eternal Lord&quot; or &quot;Sovereign of the Universe&quot;, is a hymn of the Jewish liturgy.</blockquote><a href="/songs/adon-olam">Adon Olam</a></li><li><blockquote>In various rites it shares with <b>Adon Olam</b> the place of honour at the opening of the morning service and the close of the evening service.</blockquote><a href="/songs/yigdal">Yigdal</a></li></ul></section>
